      : `Paddle Today tracks ${group.routeCount} ${group.name} ${routeLabel} across ${regionSummary}. Compare the route cards on this page, then open an individual route for put-in, take-out, distance, and access notes.`,
  },
  {
    question: `How should I check ${group.name} water levels?`,
    answer: isMerrimackHub
      ? 'The 400 cfs runnable floor and 1,500–2,000 cfs preferred range belong to the documented Franklin–Concord run and its USGS 01081500 gauge near Franklin Junction. Lower-valley trips are planning-only; that upstream station is context and does not provide a verified route-specific threshold for each downstream reach.'
      : isUpperCumberlandHub
      ? 'USGS 03404000 at Williamsburg is the reference for these routes. KDFWR publishes 3.0–3.8 ft (300–700 cfs) for the Upper Cumberland access chain; its separate Cumberland gorge guidance calls 500–1,100 cfs best and up to 2,000 cfs challenging for skilled paddlers. Use the band and hazards on the selected trip because the gorge routes have a different character.'
      : isTradewaterHub
      ? 'KDFWR rates 10–15 ft and 5–50 cfs as good at USGS 03384100 near Providence for this lower corridor. Its separate Olney ranges apply farther upstream. The river can rise quickly after rain, and the trip card shows whether the Providence station is direct or proxy context for that route.'
      : isLevisaForkHub
      ? 'KDFWR publishes separate recommendations for Pikeville and Prestonsburg. Use USGS 03209500 near Pikeville for the upper trail’s 800–1,400 cfs / 7.4–8.5 ft band, and USGS 03209800 at Prestonsburg for the downstream 800–1,500 cfs / 3.5–5.0 ft band. These are local recommendations, not interchangeable safety limits; check the station attached to the selected trip.'
      : isLittleForkHub
      ? 'Minnesota DNR says Little Fork flow usually peaks in late April and falls through summer; a reading of 3 feet or lower at the river-mile-21.6 USGS gauge means most rapids are too shallow for easy passage. The trip cards use reach-specific Minnesota DNR gauge stations, so check the station shown for the exact route and watch for rapid changes.'
      : isCurrentRiverHub
      ? 'Use the gauge on the selected trip: USGS 07064533 above Akers for upper Current options and USGS 07067000 at Van Buren for lower Current options. The card floors (230 or 300 cfs upper; 700 cfs lower) are conservative community planning cues, not NPS safety limits. Check the park’s current river levels and closure notices; NPS closure stages use gauge height in feet and are a separate check.'
      : isJacksForkHub
      ? 'Use the station shown on the selected trip: USGS 07065200 near Mountain View for upper reaches, USGS 07065495 at Alley Spring for trips in the middle corridor, and USGS 07066000 at Eminence for the lower finish. NPS warns that parts of the upper river are navigable only at certain times because of low water. The 100 or 200 cfs card floors are conservative community-sourced planning cues, not NPS safe-flow rules; no ideal range or high-flow cutoff is claimed.'
      : isElevenPointHub
      ? 'Paddle Today shows USGS 07071500 near Bardley as conservative same-river context and uses a minimum-only low-water cue; there is no endorsed ideal range or high-flow cutoff for every section. The gauge is downstream of Greer Spring, so it does not describe the smaller upper river by itself. Check the live trend, recent rain, and the selected access and hazard notes before launch.'
      : isVillageCreekHub
      ? 'Use USGS 08041500 near Kountze with the local guidance from Big Thicket National Preserve: below about 3 feet, expect exposed sandbars and logs; at 10 feet or higher, swift current can be hazardous. Village Creek is free-flowing and responds to rain, so check the gauge trend and flood notices before launch; these are planning cues, not an all-clear.'
      : isWabashHub
      ? 'Wells County Trails calls 1.5–3 feet the ideal kayaking height, warns that low water may require dragging, and says to stay off the river during Action or Flood Stage. Check USGS 03323000 at Bluffton and the local flood-stage status before setting out; a gauge reading alone is not a safety guarantee.'
      : isSpokaneHub
      ? 'Use USGS 12419000 near Post Falls for the short Harvard–Barker section; it is upstream context for the rest of the upper river and does not confirm local water near Barker and Sullivan. USGS 12422500 at Spokane provides lower-river context. The TJ Meenach–Plese expert run, upper combinations beyond Barker, and the Nine Mile pool are planning-only because their conditions cannot be represented safely by one live score.'
      : isSaludaHub
      ? 'USGS 02168504 measures the river below Lake Murray Dam. SCDNR says daily flows may range from 400 to 20,000 cfs and warns that releases can change conditions rapidly; that broad range is not a route-specific paddling target. Check the live trend and release notices, and treat the gauge as context rather than an all-clear.'
      : isBroadRiverHub
      ? 'Check the gauge and source attached to the exact trip. Ninety-Nine Islands–Daltons uses a stage reading in feet at USGS 02153551; Peak–Harbison and the lower sections use discharge readings at their own gauges. These locations and units do not form one river-wide threshold, and a gauge reading does not clear dam portages or access conditions.'
      : isAnimasHub
      ? 'Check the station and flow notes for the selected trip. Cedar Hill–Aztec Riverside uses USGS 09364010 below Aztec; downstream choices use direct stations near Penny Lane or Farmington depending on the endpoints. Farmington says the Animas is too low for rafting below 600 cfs, which is a rafting planning cue rather than a safety limit for every craft or reach. Check the live reading, trend, weather, sediment, and access notices.'
      : isSanJuanHub
      ? 'Use the gauge attached to the exact trip: USGS 09355500 near Archuleta for the upper reaches and USGS 09365000 at Farmington for the lower corridor. Southwest Paddler’s 300 cfs minimum and 500 cfs target are community planning cues, not safety limits. Reclamation’s 500–1,000 cfs base-flow target is a weekly habitat objective downstream toward Lake Powell, not paddling guidance. Check current gauge trends, Navajo Dam notices, weather, and local hazards.'
      : isCrowWingHub
      ? 'Use the gauge shown on the selected trip. Minnesota DNR’s interpreted Nimrod gauge provides context for the Huntersville-to-Cottingham corridor; the Old Wadena–Green Oak and Green Oak–Lake Placid trips use the separate Sylvan Dam station. The stations are in different parts of the river, so do not apply one reading or threshold to every section. Check the live trend and local conditions.'
      : isChattahoocheeHub
      ? 'Use the gauge assigned to the exact put-in in the NPS River Flow Rate table; its five USGS stations cover different launch zones. NPS recommends canoes and kayaks stay below 4,000 cfs and says no water-based activities are recommended above 8,000 cfs. Buford and Morgan Falls releases, storms, and rapid changes can override a static reading, so check the current trend and release schedule too.'
      : isHousatonicHub
      ? 'Use the gauge attached to the selected trip: Lenoxdale for the upper chain, Great Barrington near Division Street for the Stockbridge and Great Barrington starts, and Ashley Falls for the downstream Sheffield reaches. HVA’s broad 150 cfs minimum is a low-water planning cue, not a safe-flow guarantee; dams, intervening tributaries, water quality, and local conditions still matter.'
      : isLoupRiverHub
      ? 'USGS 06793000 near Genoa provides live Loup River context. Nebraska Game and Parks’ 350 cfs field test supports a minimum-only cue for the upper Syas–Monroe section; it is not a threshold for the whole trail. ADM options continue onto the Platte and are planning-only.'
      : isVerdeRiverHub
      ? 'Use the guidance for the exact reach. Clarkdale discourages boating at 300 cfs and above and blocks launch areas at 1,000 cfs and above on Lower TAPCO–Tuzigoot. The only scored trip is the community-sourced 9-mile White Bridge–Beasley Flat range of 150–1,000 cfs at the Camp Verde gauge; it is not a safety limit. RiverBrain’s Beasley Flat–Childs figures are planning references for that expert-only run. Other endpoint combinations have no route-specific numeric band.'
      : isAmericanLowerHub
      ? 'Use USGS 11446500 at Fair Oaks and review the trend and current releases. The 1,400–3,100 cfs comfortable band and 5,000 cfs maximum reference are community-derived planning cues, not official safe-water limits.'
      : isRussianRiverHub
      ? 'Use the guide for your exact stretch. California Creeks ties its 300–1,500 cfs guidance for Healdsburg-to-near-Forestville and Alexander-to-Healdsburg runs to the Healdsburg gauge. Dreamflows separately lists 500–2,500 cfs triggers for Healdsburg-to-Guerneville at Hacienda Bridge. These are community flow indicators, not safe limits; the Asti reach has separate seasonal guidance.'
      : isGreenRiverHub
      ? 'Use the source for your exact zone and do not compare the numbers directly: the Upper Green guide uses Green River Lake dam release in cfs, KDFWR lists a separate Greensburg stage gauge in feet and a Munfordville discharge gauge in cfs, and NPS gives Mammoth Cave stage ranges and a park closure threshold. Multi-zone combinations are planning-only because one reading cannot represent all of those conditions.'
      : isMillersHub
      ? 'USGS 01166500 at Erving provides direct flow context for the Lower Millers run. The Athol–Orange Blue Trail is flatwater held by the Orange dam, so the downstream Erving gauge does not describe that reach. Trips that cross the dam are planning-only; verify both sections and the portage separately.'
      : isBayouDeViewHub
      ? 'Use the live USGS 07077730 gauge near Brinkley. AGFC says the trail needs a stage around 14 ft or higher, warns that high water is dangerous, and separately says the Dagmar Road gate closes at 17 ft. The gate closure is an access restriction, not an upper safe-water limit; check the roads and seasonal Apple Lake closure before launch.'
      : isWillametteHub
      ? 'The Willamette Kayak and Canoe Club’s 2,000 cfs low, 6,000 cfs optimal, and 20,000 cfs high references apply to the Alton Baker–Harrisburg reach and two shorter options within it. The other trips are planning-only because no route-specific numeric range is verified; their Harrisburg or Corvallis USGS readings are context, not a live recommendation.'
      : isTruckeeHub
      ? 'Use the gauge and flow notes attached to your selected route. USGS stations at Farad, Reno, Vista, and below Derby Dam cover different parts of the river. Truckee flow is regulated and varies by season; one station does not represent every reach or clear a dam portage, whitewater feature, or access restriction.'
      : isFarmingtonHub
      ? 'Use USGS 01186000 at Riverton for these upper West Branch trips. Riverton’s 250 cfs minimum and 360–980 cfs optimum for downriver canoeing are community planning cues for this corridor, not safety guarantees or guidance for the lower Farmington. Check the live trend, release notices, weather, and access before launch.'
      : isSusquehannaHub
      ? 'Use the gauge named on the selected route. Pennsylvania North Branch maps reference Towanda, Meshoppen, Wilkes-Barre, Bloomsburg, and Danville; New York trips use separate local gauge context. These readings describe different reaches, not one river-wide threshold. Check the live trend, weather, access, and current notices.'
      : scoredRouteCount
      ? `Check the gauge notes for your stretch and its live score where available. This hub uses ${gaugeNames.length === 1 ? gaugeNames[0] : `${gaugeNames.length} gauges or gauge references`}. Planning routes have no live score; check their source guidance and current access notices.`
      : `These planning routes have no live conditions score. Use each route’s gauge notes and source guidance, then check current waterway notices, weather, access, and local conditions before launching.`,
  },
  {
    question: scoredRouteCount ? `Which ${group.name} route is best today?` : `How should I choose a ${group.name} trip?`,
    answer: isMerrimackHub
      ? 'Pick the reach first. Use the five documented Franklin–Concord options for the published upper-river flow guidance, or compare lower-valley trips by endpoint, distance, current, and dam boundaries. Allenstown’s Pelissier Boat Launch requires a resident permit sticker, so visitors should choose another take-out.'
      : isSpokaneHub
      ? 'Choose by the hardest water on the route. The Water Trail describes Barker–Mirabeau as Class II for more experienced paddlers and TJ Meenach–Plese as expert-only Class III–IV. Plese Flats–Nine Mile Dam is a separate flatwater option. The City prohibits boating between the Division and Monroe Street bridges; do not extend a trip upstream into that corridor.'
      : isSaludaHub
      ? 'The single trip covers the same 3.5-mile reach from either public upstream ramp to Gardendale, a carry-in access. Major rapids begin downstream of I-26, but SCDNR does not list the bridge as a public river landing; do not use it as an assumed take-out.'
      : isSusquehannaHub
      ? 'Choose the river reach first: upper main-stem options in New York or North Branch sections in Pennsylvania. The picker keeps alternate endpoint pairs available, and each trip retains its own mileage, access, gauge, and hazard details.'
      : scoredRouteCount
      ? isHousatonicHub
        ? 'Choose a reach by its own access and difficulty. The upper technical section starts with the mandatory Woods Pond Dam portage; the two Glendale reaches are separated by a separate land portage; the lower picker options have different launch grades, parking, and shuttle lengths. Use the trip details before selecting a route.'
        : isSanJuanHub
        ? 'Choose one access section at a time when starting out. The upper Navajo Dam tailwater differs from the lower river’s diversion and fence hazards, and the Farmington city segments have posted public access rules. The longer trips remain in the picker with intermediate access notes; check the exact route and gauge before setting a shuttle.'
        : isTruckeeHub
        ? 'Start with one of the six documented sections below. The Floriston reach is technical whitewater; Reno–Sparks routes include urban access and whitewater-park features; the lower Truckee includes a required Derby Dam portage and a tribal permit for the Wadsworth take-out. The other 18 endpoint combinations remain available in the picker with their own route notes.'
        : `Compare current scores where available, along with water level, recent trend, weather, access, and route difficulty. Planning-only cards provide trip information without a live paddling recommendation.`
      : `Compare distance, difficulty, launch access, and shuttle needs. These routes are planning references; verify current conditions with the linked sources before choosing a trip.`,
  },
];
const siteUrl = Astro.site?.href ?? 'https://paddletoday.com/';
const homeUrl = new URL('/', siteUrl).href;
const groupUrl = new URL(`/rivers/by-river/${group.riverId}/`, siteUrl).href;
const groupStructuredData = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    '@id': `${groupUrl}#breadcrumbs`,
    itemListElement: [
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 1,
        name: 'Home',
        item: homeUrl,
      },
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 2,
        name: group.name,
        item: groupUrl,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    '@id': `${groupUrl}#faq`,
    mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: item.question,
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: item.answer,
      },
    })),
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={groupTitle}
  description={groupDescription}
  structuredData={groupStructuredData}
  bodyClass="page-river-hub"
>
  <article class="river-group-page" data-river-group-page data-river-id={group.riverId} data-river-name={group.name} data-river-state={stateSummary} data-river-region={regionSummary} data-has-consolidated-trips={hasConsolidatedTrips ? 'true' : 'false'}>
    <section class={`river-group-page__hero${heroPhoto ? ' river-group-page__hero--photo' : ' river-group-page__hero--fallback'}`}>
      <div class="river-group-page__hero-main">
        <p class="eyebrow river-group-page__hero-kicker">
          <span>River guide</span>
          <span aria-hidden="true">·</span>
          <span>{stateDisplay}</span>
        </p>
        <h1 class="river-group-page__title">{group.name}</h1>
        <p class="river-group-page__lede" data-group-headline>
          {isBroadRiverHub
            ? 'Start with the eight South Carolina trips named by Paddle SC, then use the picker to compare alternate endpoint pairs. These routes cross different gauge areas and include the Ninety-Nine Islands, Lockhart, and Neal Shoals dam boundaries; check the source and access notes for your exact trip.'
            : isMerrimackHub
            ? 'Choose the documented Franklin–Concord trips for published flow guidance, or compare lower-valley access pairs from Boscawen through Hooksett. The Franklin gauge and flow range do not apply to every downstream option, and the Allenstown Ferry Street launch requires a resident permit.'
            : isHousatonicHub
            ? 'Plan the Berkshire Housatonic by reach: the upper mainstem and Woods Pond portage, separate trips above and below Glendale Dam, and the Great Barrington–Sheffield access chain. Gauge context, skill, and launch access change by section, so compare the selected trip’s details before planning a shuttle.'
            : isSanJuanHub
            ? 'Follow the San Juan from Navajo Dam through Blanco and Vereda del Rio to Farmington and Kirtland. Begin with a named section, then compare longer choices with intermediate access, separate gauges, release changes, and diversion hazards.'
            : isSusquehannaHub
            ? 'This guide covers the upper main stem in New York and the North Branch in Pennsylvania. Start with a documented North Branch section below, then compare endpoint choices; gauge and conditions must match the exact reach.'
            : isChattahoocheeHub
            ? 'Choose a source-backed Chattahoochee section, then verify the USGS gauge assigned to your put-in. Buford and Morgan Falls Dam releases can change flow quickly, and the NPS limits vary by craft.'
            : isLittleForkHub
            ? 'Choose one of the two DNR map sections, then select the exact launch and take-out. The ten trips include the official 15.2-mile Veterans Park–Highway 73 day trip, the 13.1-mile Dentaybow–Fiedler full-day trip, and shorter or longer access combinations with reach-specific gauge context.'
            : isWillimanticHub
            ? 'Compare five selected Willimantic trips across three distinct stretches: upper quickwater, the Eagleville impoundment, and the river below the dam. Each stretch has different access, gauge cues, and hazards.'
            : isCedarRiverAdirondacksHub
            ? 'Compare two different Adirondack trips: the Cedar River Flow out-and-back above Wakely Dam and the advanced Pelon Road to Outer Gooley wilderness reach. Their access, gauge context, portages, and egress needs differ, so review the selected trip details before planning.'
            : isCedarRiverHub
            ? 'Follow the connected Cedar River from the Austin area in Minnesota into Iowa. The route choices span different gauges, dams, portages, and public-access systems, so use the details for your exact section.'
            : isLeafRiverHub
            ? 'Start with the designated Upper Leaf Blueway from Eastabuchie to Chain Park and the Middle Blueway from Chain Park to Sims Road. Highway 588 and 590 launch options and longer endpoint combinations remain in the trip picker; check exact route access, distance, and gauge context before planning.'
            : isBlackstoneHub
            ? 'Follow the Blackstone access chain from Rivers Edge and Manville through Sycamore and Albion toward Kelly House and Pratt. Albion and Pratt require careful portage planning; confirm the access notes and exact endpoints for the selected reach.'
            : isPascagoulaHub
            ? 'Start with a short access section in the George County Pascagoula blueway, then compare longer options that share intermediate ramps. The Highway 63 to River Park reach is farther downstream in Jackson County; check the exact launch, take-out, gauge context, and wind or tide notes for your trip.'
            : isKennebecHub
            ? 'Choose a reach by area: upper sections around Solon, Madison, Skowhegan, and Norridgewock; central options through Waterville, Sidney, and Augusta; or lower trips toward Bath and Phippsburg. North Sidney is a proxy gauge upstream, while the Gardiner station and tide or wind checks are more relevant downstream.'
            : isPenobscotHub
            ? 'Plan by river section: North-central reaches around T3 R11, Medway, Lincoln, Howland, and Passadumkeag use West Enfield gauge context; the Bangor Valley and Bangor-to-Verona reach use the Bangor gauge and add tide, wind, and marine traffic. Check the exact access and take-out notes.'
            : isBlackCreekHub
            ? 'Start with the six documented Black Creek reaches from Churchwell Road through Fairley Bridge. The trip picker also includes longer landing combinations, with intermediate public access points for breaks or shorter exits.'
            : isLamoilleHub
            ? 'Choose a named Lamoille Paddlers’ Trail reach, then check its specific portages, water type, and access. The trail spans quiet water, rock gardens, falls, and Arrowhead Lake; a Johnson gauge reading does not describe every route hazard.'
            : isWoonasquatucketHub
            ? 'Plan the upper Georgiaville–Greystone paddle and the lower Manton–Providence paddles as separate zones. The WRWC guide describes an unverified access and portage gap between them; check the source notes before planning a longer trip.'
            : isBayouTecheHub
            ? 'Choose a town-to-town dock section on Louisiana’s Bayou Teche National Paddle Trail. Start with the named legs below, then compare longer itineraries in the picker. The upper and downstream trail sections have different portage and access needs.'
            : isSuwanneeHub
            ? 'Choose a named Suwannee River Wilderness Trail section, then check its access and overnight plan. Start with the source-backed day legs below; longer endpoint combinations stay available in the trip picker.'
            : isSaukRiverHub
            ? `Start with the DNR-recommended day trips or the documented Frogtown extension, then compare all ${group.routeCount} access pairs. The upper chain-of-lakes, the St. Martin gauge area, and the lower rapids have different conditions and planning needs.`
            : isTruckeeHub
            ? 'Choose a documented reach first, then compare nearby endpoint pairs. The upper Floriston–Verdi gorge, Reno–Sparks whitewater corridor, and lower river each have different difficulty, gauge context, and access requirements.'
            : isFarmingtonHub
            ? 'Choose the Riverton–People’s Forest reach, the People’s Forest–Route 181/318 reach, or the combined run. The selector keeps all three choices together so you can compare endpoints and shuttle needs on one guide.'
            : isFloydsForkHub
            ? 'Follow the six KDFWR-listed access legs from North Beckley to Cliffside, or choose the separately published full-corridor trip. The picker keeps every endpoint combination available with its own mileage, access, gauge, and hazard notes.'
            : `Plan a paddle across ${group.regions.length} ${areaLabel}. ${scoredRouteCount ? 'Compare distance, difficulty, and current scores where available before choosing your stretch.' : 'Compare distance, difficulty, and access. These planning routes have no live conditions score.'}`}
        </p>

        <div class="river-group-page__hero-facts" aria-label={`${group.name} trip overview`}>
          <div class="river-group-page__hero-fact">
            <strong>{group.routeCount}</strong>
            <span>mapped trips</span>
          </div>
          {scoredRouteCount > 0 ? <div class="river-group-page__hero-fact"><strong>{scoredRouteCount}</strong><span>scored today</span></div> : null}
          {planningRouteCount > 0 ? <div class="river-group-page__hero-fact"><strong>{planningRouteCount}</strong><span>planning routes</span></div> : null}
          {distanceRangeLabel ? (
            <div class="river-group-page__hero-fact">
              <strong>{distanceRangeLabel}</strong>
              <span>distance range</span>
            </div>
          ) : null}
          {difficultyLabels.length > 0 ? (
            <div class="river-group-page__hero-fact">
              <strong>{difficultyLabels.join(' · ')}</strong>
              <span>difficulty options</span>
            </div>
          ) : null}
        </div>
      </div>
      {heroPhoto ? (
        <figure class="river-group-page__hero-media">
          <img
            src={heroPhoto.src}
            alt={heroPhoto.alt}
            width={heroPhoto.width}
            height={heroPhoto.height}
            loading="eager"
            decoding="async"
            fetchpriority="high"
          />
          <figcaption>
            <strong>{heroPhoto.caption}</strong>
            <span>
              Photo by {heroPhoto.sourceUrl ? (
                <a href={heroPhoto.sourceUrl} rel="noopener">{heroPhoto.credit}</a>
              ) : heroPhoto.credit}
              {' · '}
              {heroPhoto.licenseUrl ? (
                <a href={heroPhoto.licenseUrl} rel="noopener">{heroPhoto.licenseLabel}</a>
              ) : heroPhoto.licenseLabel}
            </span>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      ) : null}
    </section>

    {isSpokaneHub ? (
      <section class="river-group-page__panel" aria-labelledby="spokane-river-sections-title">
        <div class="river-group-page__section-head">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow section-label">Spokane River Water Trail</p>
            <h2 class="section-title" id="spokane-river-sections-title">Choose a Spokane trip by its reach</h2>
          </div>
          <p class="muted river-group-page__section-note">
            The six focused guides follow changes in skill, water and access. Longer combinations stay available in the trip picker.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="river-hub-context__grid">
          <article class="river-hub-context__item">
            <h3>Upper river · Harvard to Boulder Beach</h3>
            <p>
              The Water Trail separates the novice-friendly Harvard–Barker Class I reach, the more experienced Barker–Mirabeau Class II run, and the mostly flatwater sections beyond Mirabeau. USGS 12419000 near Post Falls is upstream context. A 2025 technical report documented a dry reach between Barker and Sullivan, so check local water instead of relying on that upstream reading alone.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li><a href={routeSlugHref('spokane-river-harvard-barker')}>Harvard Road to Barker Road</a> · 2 mi · Class I</li>
              <li><a href={routeSlugHref('spokane-river-barker-mirabeau')}>Barker Road to Mirabeau</a> · 4.5 mi · Class II</li>
              <li><a href={routeSlugHref('spokane-river-mirabeau-plantes-ferry')}>Mirabeau to Plante’s Ferry</a> · 1 mi · mostly flatwater</li>
              <li><a href={routeSlugHref('spokane-river-plantes-ferry-boulder-beach')}>Plante’s Ferry to Boulder Beach</a> · 4.5 mi · mostly flatwater, with Coyote Rock</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="river-hub-context__item">
            <h3>Lower river · Redband and TJ Meenach</h3>
            <p>
              Redband to TJ Meenach is a short urban option. The Water Trail describes the TJ Meenach–Plese Flats section as roughly seven miles of Class III–IV whitewater for equipped experts; less experienced paddlers should exit at TJ. The listed flows are broad paddling references, not a safe range for every boater.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li><a href={routeSlugHref('spokane-river-redband-tj-meenach')}>Redband Park to TJ Meenach</a></li>
              <li><a href={routeSlugHref('spokane-river-tj-meenach-plese-flats')}>TJ Meenach to Plese Flats</a> · about 7 mi · expert Class III–IV</li>
            </ul>
            <p>The City prohibits boating between the Division Street and Monroe Street bridges. Do not extend an upstream trip into that restricted corridor.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="river-hub-context__item">
            <h3>Nine Mile · flatwater above the dam</h3>
            <p>
              Plese Flats to the Nine Mile Dam take-out is a separate five-mile flatwater trip. The Water Trail says to exit on river left before the dam. USGS 12422500 at Spokane provides broader upstream context; dam operations and local pool conditions need a same-day check.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li><a href={routeSlugHref('spokane-river-plese-flats-nine-mile-dam')}>Plese Flats to Nine Mile Dam</a> · 5 mi · flatwater</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
        </div>
        <p>
          Review the <a href="https://www.spokaneriver.net/watertrail/paddling-stateline-to-upriver-dam/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">upper Water Trail section guide</a>, its <a href="https://www.spokaneriver.net/watertrail/paddling-water-street-to-plese-flats/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Water Street–Plese Flats safety guide</a>, the <a href="https://www.spokaneriver.net/watertrail/paddling-plese-flats-to-little-falls-dam/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Plese Flats–Nine Mile section guide</a>, <a href="https://my.spokanecity.org/rulesoftheriver/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">City river rules</a>, and the <a href="https://www.spokaneaquifer.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/FINAL_GSI_TM_SAJB-Model-Development_20251212.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2025 low-flow observation report</a> before choosing a trip.
        </p>
      </section>
    ) : null}

    {isSaludaHub ? (
