        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="river-hub-context__grid">
        <article class="river-hub-context__item">
          <h3>Water levels</h3>
          <p>
            {isYellowBreechesHub
              ? 'Use USGS 01571500 at Camp Hill as same-creek context. Cumberland County recommends 1.4–2.0 ft for boating and lists 6 ft as action stage and 7 ft as flood stage; the gauge does not guarantee conditions at each access point.'
              : isSaltRiverHub
              ? 'USGS 09502000 below Stewart Mountain Dam is direct telemetry for this Lower Salt reach. HikeArizona gives 500 cfs as a community minimum and 1,000 cfs as desired, but does not publish an upper cutoff; check live trend, current advisories, and local conditions.'
              : isCurrentRiverHub
              ? 'The Current River guide separates the Upper Current between Cedar Grove and Round Spring from the Lower Current around Waymeyer, Van Buren, Big Spring, Cataract, and Gooseneck. USGS 07064533 above Akers and USGS 07067000 at Van Buren describe different reaches; select the gauge shown on the exact trip card.'
              : isJacksForkHub
              ? 'Jacks Fork options follow one main access corridor, but the upper section is seasonal and trip guidance uses three different stations. The trip cards group every endpoint choice here; use the direct source-backed adjacent floats to anchor a plan, and select the route that matches your launch, take-out, time, and gauge.'
              : isElevenPointHub
              ? 'Use USGS 07071500 near Bardley only as conservative same-river context. The station is downstream of Greer Spring, so the smaller Thomasville–Cane Bluff section can be shallow even when the lower gauge looks usable. Current route cards show a minimum-only planning cue, not an ideal range or high-water clearance; check rainfall, the live trend, and conditions at the actual put-in.'
              : isSpokaneHub
              ? 'Use the station named for the chosen section. USGS 12419000 near Post Falls gives direct flow telemetry for Harvard–Barker, while the Spokane reading is lower-river context. Neither gauge verifies the dry-prone Barker–Sullivan channel or the Nine Mile pool above its dam; inspect those places locally.'
              : isSaludaHub
              ? 'USGS 02168504 is below Lake Murray Dam and provides direct flow telemetry for this upper reach. SCDNR publishes a broad daily range of 400–20,000 cfs and warns of rapid release changes; no route-specific scoring band is inferred from that range.'
              : gaugeNames.length === 1
              ? `These ${group.name} routes use ${gaugeNames[0]} as the main gauge reference.`
              : `These ${group.name} routes use ${gaugeNames.length} gauge references depending on the stretch.`}
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="river-hub-context__item">
          <h3>Route options</h3>
          <p>
            {isYellowBreechesHub
              ? `Compare all ${group.routeCount} Yellow Breeches endpoint choices from Simpson Park through McCormick Park, Lower Allen, and Liberty Forge to B6. Six focused trips have dedicated route guides; overlapping combinations remain available here, and the adjacent Simpson–McCormick leg is available as a short option.`
              : isSaltRiverHub
              ? `Compare all ${group.routeCount} Lower Salt endpoint choices from Water Users through Blue Point, Goldfield, and Phon D. Sutton to Granite Reef. Four adjacent sections have dedicated pages; six longer combinations remain selectable here with their own endpoints, mileage, access notes, and shuttle details.`
              : isCurrentRiverHub
              ? `Compare all ${group.routeCount} Current River endpoint choices here. The picker keeps the two NPS-described sections separate and preserves every trip with its own endpoints, gauge, distance, shuttle, camping, and safety details.`
              : isJacksForkHub
              ? `Compare all ${group.routeCount} Jacks Fork endpoint choices here. The NPS adjacent float sections provide useful anchors, while longer combinations remain available with their own mileage, shuttle, camping, and hazard notes. Use the gauge assigned to the specific trip: Mountain View upstream, Alley Spring in the middle, or Eminence for the lower finish.`
              : isElevenPointHub
              ? `Compare all ${group.routeCount} public endpoint choices here, from the upper reaches through Turner Mill, Whitten, Riverton, and The Narrows. The route cards keep their own access pairs, mileage, and safety notes together; choose trips within the river section and gauge context you plan to use.`
              : isRussianRiverHub
              ? `Compare all ${group.routeCount} endpoint combinations here. Six source-backed trips keep standalone pages; the remaining options stay selectable with their own mileage, access, shuttle, and hazard notes. The upper, middle, and lower reaches use different guide and gauge references.`
              : isSpokaneHub
              ? `Compare all ${group.routeCount} Spokane River options here. Six focused Water Trail sections keep standalone guides; the other 20 endpoint combinations remain available as cards with their own access and hazard notes. Upper Post Falls telemetry, the lower Spokane gauge, and the Nine Mile pool describe different conditions.`
              : isSaludaHub
              ? 'One trip card represents the 3.5-mile reach from two alternative public launch ramps to the Gardendale carry-in take-out. The short cross-bank pair and I-26 endpoint combinations are not listed as trips because they add little route value or rely on an undocumented take-out.'
              : isGreenRiverHub
              ? `Compare all ${group.routeCount} Green River trip choices across the Upper Green, Hart County, and Mammoth Cave. Eight source-backed trips keep standalone pages; the other 24 remain available here with their endpoint, mileage, access, and hazard details. Eleven cross-zone combinations are planning-only because their gauge guidance cannot be combined into one route score.`
              : isBlackfootHub
              ? `Compare all ${group.routeCount} endpoint combinations here. Five source-documented runs keep standalone pages; the other 31 remain selectable as trip options. Route-specific flow scores are limited to those five named reaches; the rest show the Bonner gauge as context only.`
              : isAnimasHub
              ? `Compare all ${group.routeCount} Animas trip choices here. Five source-backed sections keep standalone pages; the five composite options remain selectable with their own endpoints, mileage, bailout access, gauge references, and hazards.`
              : isSanJuanHub
              ? `Compare all ${group.routeCount} San Juan trip choices here. Five named access sections keep standalone pages; six longer combinations remain selectable with their own endpoints, mileage, intermediate access, gauge references, and hazards.`
              : isChattahoocheeHub
              ? `Compare all ${group.routeCount} curated Chattahoochee route guides here. Eight focused recreation-area sections cover the NPS access chain; Powers Island–Paces Mill and the Upper Chattahoochee and Columbus whitewater runs remain separate because they describe different paddling contexts. Other endpoint combinations now lead to this section guide.`
              : isBarrenRiverHub
              ? `Compare all ${group.routeCount} Lower Barren River choices here. Five individual KDFWR access segments keep standalone pages; five composite endpoint combinations remain selectable with their own mileage, access, gauge, and hazard details. Finney stage and Bowling Green discharge apply to different route sections.`
              : isUpperCumberlandHub
              ? `Compare all ${group.routeCount} Upper Cumberland choices here. The three consecutive access legs keep focused route pages; longer combinations remain available as trip cards. Williamsburg-to-Redbird and the downstream Cumberland Falls approaches retain their own pages because the gorge has different flow guidance and hazards.`
              : isTradewaterHub
              ? `Compare all ${group.routeCount} lower Tradewater choices here. Four consecutive KDFWR access sections keep standalone guides; five longer endpoint combinations remain selectable with their own distance, access, and trip-specific gauge context.`
              : isLittleForkHub
              ? `Compare all ${group.routeCount} Little Fork trip choices here. Seven reach-specific guides keep standalone pages; three composite endpoint combinations open their exact trip cards on this hub. All ten choices retain their own endpoints, distances, access details, and gauge assignments.`
              : isHousatonicHub
              ? `Compare all ${group.routeCount} Berkshire Housatonic trip choices here. Nine reach-specific sections keep standalone pages; six longer endpoint combinations remain selectable with their own access, mileage, gauge, portage, and safety details.`
              : isWillimanticHub
